VulkanNetwork - reusable C++17 WinSock2/POSIX UDP and reliable byte messages.
  network/network.hpp       umbrella API
  network/endpoint.*        IPv4 and IPv6 address resolution and comparison
  network/socket.*          nonblocking UDP socket and timed readability
  network/stream.*          bounded fragmentation, ACKs, retransmission, pacing
  network/session.*         channels over a caller-owned UDP socket
  network/host.*            dedicated-socket adapter for network::session

A game can route its own movement/weapon packets through one UDP socket and
send independent reliable scene bytes using a net::session over that socket.
Feed VSN1 datagrams to session.accept(peer,packet,size), call session.pump(socket)
to dispatch data and ACKs, and session.pop(delivery) for complete byte messages.
Game-specific decoding, session tokens, authorization and scene revisions stay
with the consumer. When allow_unknown_peers=false, call open() after application
authentication. host provides start/send/pump/pop on its own socket.

Each channel imposes payload/queue/window limits and an AIMD-style send window.
Delivery queues have configurable memory caps; fragment timeouts/retries and
duplicate suppression are bounded. The default UDP bind is IPv4; explicitly
bind "::" for IPv6/dual-stack when supported. Both peers must use the same
stream::settings.mtu and stream protocol. Existing VSCN game packets are NOT
compatible with this transport; both sides must be upgraded together.

SECURITY: This is UNENCRYPTED and unauthenticated transport. FNV checksums are
not MACs. Do not expose sensitive data or trust Internet peers without adding
audited authenticated encryption (e.g. DTLS or QUIC) and rate-limit/access rules.
No guaranteed throughput or latency exists on arbitrary networks. The current
AIMD is a starting point, not a standards-grade congestion controller or
substitute for loss, reordering, abuse and soak testing.

